WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:03.860
Film Courage: How do you know a protagonist
is a character you want to write about?

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:06.060
How do you know you’re really connected
to that protagonist?

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:11.100
Adam Davis, Writer/Director: Hmm…that’s
a very good question.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:19.920
I think for me it comes from a little bit
of the story and about what I want to say

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:21.270
at that time.

00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:27.090
I don’t tend to go with tropes of characters.

00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:34.449
I really go with what is the situation and
what character can be tested the most in that

00:00:34.449 --> 00:00:37.030
situation?

00:00:37.030 --> 00:00:42.329
The most recent thing that my writing partner
Will and I have done as a horror feature with

00:00:42.329 --> 00:00:52.309
a lot of social (very topical) social commentary
and that just came out of a specific very

00:00:52.309 --> 00:01:03.010
interesting idea of horror but also the fact
that right now there is a lot of disparity

00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:07.500
in terms of people’s pay just in the world.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:11.460
The rich are getting richer and the poor are
getting poorer and the middle class is disappearing

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:12.460
and everything.

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:18.820
That has been very top of mind for me so I
wanted a protagonist for this horror script

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:24.930
that really embodied that and somebody like
this blue-collar worker in Chicago that we

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:31.020
can all identify with a get behind because
I think (at least I do) but I think that we

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:35.829
all feel like we are that person right now.

00:01:35.829 --> 00:01:43.359
The corporations are just stepping down on
us and politics is stepping down on us and

00:01:43.359 --> 00:01:51.439
there is a lot of unrest and upheaval I think
and it’s one of those things that the situation

00:01:51.439 --> 00:01:58.429
can sort of dictate and it’s something that
I’m finding that having very socially conscious

00:01:58.429 --> 00:02:05.100
statements is something that I really want
to put out there because I think that I mean

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:15.370
films are a great medium that you can reach
a lot of people with and if I can put messages

00:02:15.370 --> 00:02:21.540
in there that will make people think of something
a different way like thinking about the disparity

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:27.520
between black people and white people in a
corporate environment.

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:33.580
Or with NO TOUCHING [Adam and Will’s 2016
short film] it was about the reach of misogyny.

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:41.100
I really want topical things that people can
sink their teeth into and make them think.

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:45.020
That’s my big goal with my current projects
at least.

00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:49.710
Film Courage: What were the movies for you
growing up that were like that made you sort

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:52.230
of think about social commentary?

00:02:52.230 --> 00:02:56.970
Like NORMA RAE comes to mind, that’s before
my time even, but it’s not just a feminist

00:02:56.970 --> 00:03:01.070
story but it’s a story about a small town,
people being afraid to lose their jobs, this

00:03:01.070 --> 00:03:06.663
is the only place for them to work and all
of them are sick and different things are

00:03:06.663 --> 00:03:10.520
happening but what different films growing
up did you like?

00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:16.350
Adam: Sure, I think for me what is interesting
is that it wasn’t really like dramas or

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:17.350
things like that.

00:03:17.350 --> 00:03:25.380
I found those messages in sci-fi and popcorn
films at the time so the MATRIX was like the

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:33.420
first one that really opened my opened my
eyes to the thought of what if none of this

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:35.320
is real?

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:44.490
The mind expanding aspect of looking at the
systems we have and trying to figure out how

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:50.330
much of this is real, how much of it is not
and that continues on with V FOR VENDETTA

00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:56.970
which I think is an amazing piece that again
really gets you thinking even though it’s

00:03:56.970 --> 00:03:59.130
just a comic book and action.

00:03:59.130 --> 00:04:05.710
That’s stuff that I love and am just drawn
to but the ones that feed in that social commentary

00:04:05.710 --> 00:04:06.950
are great.

00:04:06.950 --> 00:04:14.840
CHILDREN OF MEN is an example of a cautionary
tale about the future.

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:21.140
Those are the ones that I would say really
did a great job of…it’s popcorn movies

00:04:21.140 --> 00:04:26.660
but sneaking in some very important social
commentary.

00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:33.010
Film Courage: I’ve been on a Raymond Chandler
kick and I’ve been researching panels and

00:04:33.010 --> 00:04:39.450
one USC panel talked about his writings and
just how so much of noir was a commentary

00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:46.420
on the working class versus the wealthy especially
in Los Angeles during the 1930’s and corruption

00:04:46.420 --> 00:04:52.880
and paid off politicians versus the working
man.

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:59.500
It’s interesting, watching film noirs was
more mysterious but woven in is this commentary.

00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:05.490
Adam: Yes and that’s the real great Trojan
horse aspect of film is like people are there

00:05:05.490 --> 00:05:13.120
to be entertained but if you can sort of trick
them a little bit and give them a little dose

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:16.670
of social commentary they will go out like
“What was that actually about?”

00:05:16.670 --> 00:05:18.030
Or “I’m thinking about that?”

00:05:18.030 --> 00:05:19.030
It opens our eyes.

00:05:19.030 --> 00:05:20.030
That’s great.

00:05:20.030 --> 00:05:21.320
That’s a wonderful trick.

